{"id":1935,"date":"2020-12-10T12:13:33","date_gmt":"2020-12-10T20:13:33","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=1935"},"modified":"2024-09-01T13:56:25","modified_gmt":"2024-09-01T20:56:25","slug":"zumi-python-lights","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-lights\/","title":{"rendered":"1.3: Lights"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215114c20a\" name=\"6a2215114c20a\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Import libraries<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>Like every car should have, Zumi has headlights and brake lights that you can program. On a circuit board, these lights are known as\u00a0<strong>LEDs<\/strong>.<\/div>\n<div><\/div>\n<div class=\"prompt input_prompt\">In\u00a0[\u00a0]:<\/div>\n<div class=\"inner_cell\">\n<div class=\"input_area\">\n<div class=\" highlight hl-ipython3\">\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">from zumi.zumi import Zumi\r\nimport time\r\n\r\nzumi = Zumi()<\/pre>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215114c32d\" name=\"6a2215114c32d\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is an LED?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]LED stands for \u201clight-emitting diode\u201d. A diode is an electronic device that allows an electronic current to flow in only one direction. LEDs are replacing traditional incandescent light bulbs because they are more efficient.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"Zumi's-LEDs\">Zumi&#8217;s LEDs<\/h3>\n<p><span style=\"font-size: medium;\">Zumi has 8 LEDs on the board, but you can&#8217;t program all of them. There are three LEDs on Zumi&#8217;s left side that indicate the charging state, and one white LED on Zumi&#8217;s right side that indicates the Pi started successfully. There are two LEDs at the front of the board for headlights. Don&#8217;t get them confused with the Infrared (IR) sensors! You will learn about those later. The headlights are actually hidden by the shell.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1938&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]The back LEDs are much easier to see. They are the red ones on the back of Zumi.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1939&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2215114c3ac\" name=\"6a2215114c3ac\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Zumi LED Functions<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]In addition to turning the headlights and brake lights on and off, you can also use turn signals! Here are all of the functions available for you to use:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">* all_lights_on()\r\n\r\n* all_lights_off()\r\n\r\n* headlights_on()\r\n\r\n* headlights_off()\r\n\r\n* brake_lights_on()\r\n\r\n* brake_lights_off()\r\n\r\n* hazard_lights_on()\r\n\r\n* hazard_lights_off()\r\n\r\n* signal_left_on()\r\n\r\n* signal_left_off()\r\n\r\n* signal_right_on()\r\n\r\n* signal_right_off()\r\n<\/pre>\n<p>Now try incorporating turn signals into your remote control code or writing some new code. Here\u2019s an example that you can test out. Make sure you have a lot of room.<\/p>\n<p>In [ ]:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">zumi.signal_right_on()\r\nzumi.forward(duration=3)\r\nzumi.turn_right()\r\nzumi.signal_right_off()\r\nzumi.forward()<span style=\"font-size: medium;\"><code><\/code><\/span><\/pre>\n<pre><span style=\"font-size: medium;\"><code><\/code><\/span><\/pre>\n<pre><span style=\"font-size: medium;\"><code><\/code><\/span><\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 4 Import libraries [vc_column_text] Like every car should have, Zumi has headlights and brake lights that you can program. On a circuit board, these lights are known as\u00a0LEDs.&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/zumi-python-lights\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":2050,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":810,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[],"lesson-tag":[2135,2120,2233,2122,2134,2230],"class_list":["post-1935","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-led","lesson-tag-lights","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-zumi","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1935","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":8,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1935\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":9140,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/1935\/revisions\/9140"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/2050"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=1935"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\